    "pre_fix_wrong_because": "Same as index 72: the §122 surcharge was zeroed whenever the entry matched the exemplar flags standing in for China-301 list-1 or §232-aluminum membership, a gate with no basis in the §122 proclamation or the chapter 99 exception notes the rule cites.",
    "triage_notes": "Same pattern as index 72; changed lines byte-identical after chapter-prefix normalization (this instance uses `effective_from:`). Same caveats as index 72.",
    "verifier_verdict": "confirmed",
    "verifier_defect_kind": "untraceable_branch",
    "verifier_confidence": 0.85,
    "verifier_justification": "I tried to refute this as a pure identifier rename (mechanical) and as new-coverage enhancement, and both fail: the same old flag maps to different new inputs in different rules in this one diff (entry_is_line_a -> entry_is_section_122_exempt in §122 but -> entry_is_china_301_list123 in the §301 rule; entry_is_line_b -> entry_is_section_232_covered in §122, -> entry_is_section_232_aluminum in the aluminum rule, -> entry_is_china_301_2024_action in the §301 rule), so a single exemplar flag was conflating distinct memberships and the gate's meaning really changes. Reading the cited source through the pinned release (Proclamation 11012, us/rulemaking/federal-register/2026-02-25/2026-03824) shows the surcharge's only carve-outs are the Annex I/II exclusion list (\"shall not apply to imports of articles listed in paragraph 2 of Annex I\") and section 232 coverage (\"shall not apply in addition to tariffs imposed under section 232\") — nothing exempts China-301 list members, so the pre-fix zeroing branch on the §301-list flag had no basis in the provision the rule cites and its proof atoms quote. The fix re-grounds the condition on exactly the two source exceptions, which is the rubric's untraceable_branch (branch/condition with no source basis, re-grounded), and the change also alters values, not just names (PR reports 18 witness-slice cells that stop matching precisely where the old coupling was the unlawful part).",
    "verifier_quote": "-      (((((((((((((((((((((((((((((((((((((if entry_is_line_a or entry_is_line_b: 0\n+      (((((((((((((((((((((((((((((((((((((if entry_is_section_122_exempt or entry_is_section_232_covered: 0",
    "verifier_notes": "Anchor verified: the changed §122 version is indeed versions[1] (effective_from 2026-02-24, effective_to 2026-07-23) in the parent file 29dbbdac05. Its own proof atoms already cite the two lawful exceptions (\"subdivision (aa)(ii) exclusion list under heading 9903.03.03\" and \"articles of aluminum, of steel, or of copper or derivative aluminum or steel articles provided for in headings 9903.82.02 and 9903.82.04\"), so the pre-fix China-301 gate contradicted the module's own cited basis. Alternate kind worth a reviewer's eye: wrong_entity_or_scope is defensible (the exemption fired over the wrong population of lines); I chose untraceable_branch because the failure is an ungrounded condition operand that the fix re-grounds, not a mis-specified entity. Two corpus-hygiene flags: (1) this file is module.kind: composition, generator-emitted with \"hand edits prohibited\", and the identical §122/§301/aluminum/Brazil/forced-labor rewrite lands in all 100 module files of commit a9562cfd44 — one underlying defect replicated 100 times, so the corpus likely wants dedupe rather than 100 independent cases; (2) the same diff also ADDS ch75_s232_steel_heading_rate / section_232_steel_component_rate and a new stack term, which is separate new coverage (at most unrepresented_clause), not part of the confirmed §122 defect — do not let it inflate this case.",
